      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the total weight lifted by Terrell using two different weights and find out how many times Terrell must lift these weights to achieve the same total weight.\n\nLet's denote the number of lifts using the new weights as \\( n \\).\n\n**Step-by-step Approach:**\n\n1. **Calculate the Total Weight Utilized at Current Lifting:**\n   - Terrell uses two 20-pound weights. So, the total weight lifted per lift is:\n     \\[\n     2 \\times 20 = 40 \\text{ pounds per lift}\n     \\]\n   - Since Terrell performs 12 lifts with these weights, the total weight lifted is:\n     \\[\n     40 \\text{ pounds/ lift} \\times 12 \\text{ lifts} = 480 \\text{ pounds}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the Total Weight Utilized with New Weight:**\n   - Terrell now uses two 15-pound weights. So, the total weight lifted per lift is:\n     \\[\n     2 \\times 15 = 30 \\text{ pounds per lift}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Set Up the Equation to Equal Total Weight:**\n   - We want to find \\( n \\), the number of lifts using the 15-pound weights that will equal 480 pounds.\n   - Therefore, the equation to find \\( n \\) is:\n     \\[\n     30 \\text{ pounds/ lift} \\times n = 480 \\text{ pounds}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Solve the Equation:**\n   - Divide both sides of the equation by 30 pounds/ lift to isolate \\( n \\):\n     \\[\n     n = \\frac{480 \\text{ pounds}}{30 \\text{ pounds/ lift}} = 16 \\text{ lifts}\n     \\]\n\nThus, Terrell must lift the 15-pound weights 16 times to lift the same total weight as he does with the 20-pound weights.",
